<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">March on Washington</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, held on 28 August 1963, was one of the largest political demonstrations in American history: some 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial to demand civil and economic rights for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>. Organised by a coalition of civil rights, labour and religious groups led by A. Philip Randolph and Bayard Rustin, it closed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>'s 'I Have a Dream' speech. It helped build support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-on-washington.jpg" alt="March on Washington" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Center for Jewish History, NYC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:March_on_Washington_for_Jobs_and_Freedom,_Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Joachim_Prinz_1963.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
